    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the advice. i tried wiping it down but the error came right back after a few minutes. do i need to actually tip the machine or take the bottom panel off to get to the sensor? i'm worried there's water trapped somewhere i can't reach.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the error immediately returns after drying, it suggests the sensor is still detecting moisture, or the leak is happening somewhere else entirely, not just on the surface. You might need to look underneath the machine, which is where the plumbing and pump connections are, to see if any hoses are dripping.\n\nTipping the machine is usually not recommended unless the manual specifically instructs it, as it can dislodge internal components, and you might need to remove the lower kick panel or access panel to properly inspect the sensor area. If you suspect the sensor itself is faulty or the leak is internal, having a technician look at the wiring and the actual sensor housing is the safest bet.\n\nI highly recommend carefully removing the accessible bottom panel first—it's usually held by a few simple screws. This gives you a much better view of the pump housing and the drain connections without needing to tip the entire unit over. If you find standing water in the pump sump, that's likely your culprit.\n\nIt's a tricky situation, and honestly, diagnosing a persistent E15 without seeing the machine is tough. If you're unsure about disconnecting wires or manipulating the pump, stop there and call a professional.\n\nYou're dealing with a persistent",
      "source": "target",
